meta {
  name: Create Write Request
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/write-requests
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "title": "Fix patient status",
    "description": "Correct a mis-set flag",
    "connectionId": "{{connectionId}}",
    "selectSql": "SELECT id, active FROM patients WHERE id = 42",
    "writeSql": "UPDATE patients SET active = true WHERE id = 42",
    "noTransaction": false,
    "draft": false
  }
}

docs {
  Submit a write request. The server classifies `writeSql` itself:

  409 if the connection is edited while the request is being validated — nothing is run; try again.

  - **Single DML** (one INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE) — send a `selectSql` verify SELECT; it previews the affected rows.
  - **Migration** (multiple statements or any DDL, PostgreSQL/SQL Server only) — `selectSql` is ignored; the whole script runs as one transaction and rolls back atomically on failure. Set `noTransaction: true` only when the script contains a statement that can't run in a transaction (e.g. CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY, VACUUM) — it then commits statement-by-statement with NO rollback.

  On DIRECT-policy envs an authorized author executes immediately; otherwise it goes PENDING for approval.

  **Object change** — send `object: { schema, name, kind, args?, parent? }` (kind procedure | function | view | trigger) and the object's complete new definition as `writeSql`. It must be one idempotent `CREATE OR ALTER` (SQL Server) / `CREATE OR REPLACE` (PostgreSQL) of that same object, run as one batch (no `GO`). The server snapshots the definition deployed now into `snapshot` (null = the object doesn't exist yet); approval, override and submit re-read it and answer 409 `{ code: "OBJECT_CHANGED" }` if it changed since — revise to re-base. 400 when the deployed definition is hidden (encrypted / no VIEW DEFINITION). `warnings` lists anything that looks like a credential in the definition. Always transactional (`noTransaction` is ignored).

  **Job change** — send `jobChange: { jobId, action }` instead of SQL (`writeSql` is ignored; the server generates `sp_update_job` / `sp_update_jobstep` / `sp_add_jobstep` / `sp_delete_jobstep` / `sp_update_schedule` for SQL Server Agent, `cron.alter_job` for pg_cron). `action` is one of `{ type: "set_enabled", enabled }`, `{ type: "rename", name }`, `{ type: "set_description", description }`, `{ type: "edit_step", stepId, command }`, `{ type: "add_step", name, command }`, `{ type: "delete_step", stepId }`, `{ type: "set_schedule", scheduleId, msdb | cron }` (rename/description/add/delete are Agent-only). Requires the **jobEnvironments** capability for the connection's env (403 otherwise). A new or edited step command must pass as a direct write would — an EXEC/CALL, or scoped INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE statements; DDL, dynamic SQL and unscoped UPDATE/DELETE are refused (400) — and CmdExec/PowerShell/SSIS steps can't be changed. Every refusal is audited as `JOB_CHANGE_REFUSED`. The job as it is now is stored as `snapshot`; submit/approve/override answer 409 `{ code: "OBJECT_CHANGED" }` if it changed since. A job change can't be revised — cancel it and raise a new one. A schedule shared with other jobs can't be changed, and on a job that also runs steps against another database only this database's own step commands can (400).

  Set `draft: true` to save it instead: the request is stored as DRAFT and neither queued for approval nor executed, whatever the environment's policy says, until someone calls **Submit Write Request**. This is what the MCP `create_write_request` tool sends, so an agent can never reach a target database.

  503 `CONNECTION_FAILED` when a direct run can't reach the database before sending anything: nothing ran, the request is back in DRAFT, and the body carries it as `request`. Audited as `WRITE_EXECUTE_ERROR`.

  Requires a valid Bearer JWT ({{token}}).
  Requires **write mode** to be enabled.
}
